  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Where the credential lives",
      "items": [
        "tokens and secrets live in the Keychain with the right accessibility class — a token in UserDefaults is a breach on a shared device, and nothing secret reaches the logs, the bundle or a screenshot-visible screen",
        "the Domain declares the ports (SessionStore, TokenProvider); the Data target implements them over the Keychain, so no Domain code touches SecItem APIs",
        "sign out clears the Keychain and the GRDB user data — sign-out and tenant switch leave nothing behind"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "How the session starts",
      "items": [
        "Sign in with Apple, passkeys, or OIDC + PKCE in ASWebAuthenticationSession — never an embedded webview login",
        "the callback carries the authorization code and the PKCE verifier; the exchange happens at the server, not on the device",
        "the App Attest assertion is a server-side risk signal, never a client-side block — the client cannot decide that a device is honest",
        "a refresh rotates the stored token: the new one replaces the old Keychain item, and a replayed refresh is refused server-side"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "The server is authoritative",
      "items": [
        "every state-changing call is authorized server-side; a client-side route guard is not access control, and the client only hides an action it cannot perform",
        "a 403 is a rendered state (.forbidden), not a crash and not a silent retry",
        "ATS stays on; no arbitrary-loads exception without a documented reason, and no pinning without a rotation plan"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
